# FINAL HYPOTHESIS — How does age influence the likelihood of dissaving (DISSAVED)?

**Answer (natural-language hypothesis):**
In this 1989 NLSY79 young-adult cross-section (N=12,686, ages 24–32), older age is associated with a
**lower likelihood of withdrawing more from savings than putting in (dissaving)**. Each additional year of
age is associated with about 4% lower odds of dissaving (odds ratio ≈ 0.96 per year; 95% CI 0.94–0.98;
p<0.001), corresponding to a decline of roughly 0.8 percentage points per year in the probability
(average marginal effect ≈ −0.0076, SE 0.0016). The age profile is weakly non-linear (LR test for the
quadratic term p≈0.03–0.05): predicted dissaving probability rises slightly through the mid-20s
(peak ≈ age 25–26) and then declines roughly monotonically from ≈0.26 at ages 24–26 to ≈0.19 by age 32
(adjusted quadratic predictions: 0.257@24, 0.260@25, 0.248@28, 0.225@30, 0.193@32; bootstrap 95% CIs
0.23–0.28@24, 0.23–0.26@28, 0.16–0.21@32). The gradient is essentially unchanged when controlling for
income, gender, race, and BMI (unadjusted OR 0.959 vs adjusted OR 0.959) — i.e., it is not a
compositional artifact of those observed covariates.

**Fragility caveat (must accompany the claim):** The negative gradient is concentrated in the ≈31% of
observations whose INCOME equals the sample mean value 47.279 (consistent with mean-imputation; that
subgroup has far lower dissaving, 0.112 vs 0.292, and its share rises with age). Within the remaining
69% of rows the age gradient is flat-to-weakly-positive and not significant (band means 0.285→0.296→0.302),
and controlling for the imputed-flag or dropping the cluster makes the age terms non-significant.
The negative association is therefore suggestive and fragile to INCOME data quality, and must be read as
**correlational**, not causal.

**Interpretation:** Declining dissaving with age is consistent with (but not proof of) increasing
future-orientation/patience with age. The data are cross-sectional, self-reported, and cannot separate
age effects from cohort/life-cycle effects; the time-preference reading is a plausible interpretation
only, not a measured mechanism.

## Workflow
1. Inspected data (12,686×9, no missing; 23.55% dissaved).
2. Binary logistic regression of DISSAVED on age, unadjusted and adjusted (INCOME, GENDER, BLACK,
   HISPANIC, BMI); centered AGE for the quadratic (AGE_C, AGE_C2) to avoid collinearity.
3. Adjudicated linear vs quadratic via nested LR test + AIC/BIC (quadratic preferred marginally; both reported).
4. Reported OR/year (linear), AME/year and predicted P(DISSAVED) at ages 24/28/32 with delta-method
   and 100-replicate bootstrap 95% CIs.
5. Robustness: probit (identical conclusions), age banding 24–27/28–30/31–32, BMI winsorization at p99,
   INCOME imputation-cluster sensitivity (flag and drop), optional 3-category multinomial.
